The invariants tramaj is held to: portability, cheap static analysis, and determinism.

=base:main-content.cmark

# Properties of tramaj

tramaj is small on purpose. A handful of invariants are what make that
smallness pay off — they're what let a host embed the language without
trusting it, and what let an LLM emit templates without a feedback loop to
check them against.

## Small core

The whole language is one expression grammar: bindings, HAML-like elements,
`jq`-flavoured paths and calls, and a deliberately tiny builtin set. There is
no separate template phase and no macro system — an element or a fragment is
an ordinary value, so composing documents is just composing expressions.
Nothing about the identity of the language depends on HTML, a browser, or any
particular UI framework: the core (`tramaj/`) has no DOM dependency at all,
and folding a `Node` into Halogen HTML, JSON, or anything else is entirely
the host's decision.

## Portable at the boundary

Evaluation produces a generic `Node` AST with a normative JSON encoding
([`node-json.md`](/reference-node-json.html)): scalars stay scalars, and an
element may carry many opaque, structured actions rather than being tied to
one host's event model. That boundary is what lets two independent
implementations — a PureScript one and a Haskell one — target the same
grammar and share a conformance corpus, and it's what lets a host system
receive a rendered document as data rather than as a blob of host-specific
markup.

## Cheap to analyze statically

Import names, action keys, action-adaptation prefixes, and `ctx(path)` reads
are all literal strings in the syntax — never the result of evaluating an
expression. That's a deliberate restriction, not an oversight: it means a
tool can answer "what does this program import," "what actions can it
emit," "what context does it read," and "which import parameters are still
unsupplied" by walking the AST, with no evaluation step and no need to run
untrusted input to find out what it does.

## Deterministic

Templates are referentially transparent — the same bindings evaluated
against the same context values always produce the same result. Evaluation
order is unconstrained outside of binding precedence, so a host is free to
parallelize or reorder work, but never free to change the answer. This is
what makes a rendered document reproducible, cacheable, and safe to diff.

## What this buys you in practice

Put together, these properties mean a host can accept a tramaj template from
an untrusted or automated source — a CMS, a design tool, an LLM — and know,
without running it, what it can possibly do to the page or app it's
embedded in. See [Use cases](/use-cases.html) for what that looks like in
practice, or the [Reference](/reference.html) for the full specification
these properties are drawn from.

